---
// The home page. Its story is the Canopy section of Jonathan's IDM AI talk
// (Claude Design deck "IDM AI Talk (Dimagi brand)"), including the claims that
// talk's own notes say not to make: no fleet-wide kill switch, no spend limits,
// and manual-mode approval is procedure rather than a code-enforced pop-up.
// No fleet roster, live counts or figures from Dimagi's own agents, on purpose:
// the page describes the product, not who at Dimagi runs it.
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro'
import Mark from '../components/Mark.astro'
import { GITHUB_URL, SIGN_IN_URL } from '../site'

const rungs = [
  'Use AI to do things I already know how to do',
  'Use AI to do things I don’t know how to do',
  'Use AI to teach me how to do new things',
  'Improve the AI itself',
  'Help others use my improved AI',
  'Improve AI together, as a team and an org',
]

const layers = [
  ['Context', 'What the model knows at the moment of work: skills, rules and examples.'],
  ['LLM', 'Used as it is. Canopy improves everything around it.'],
  ['Harness', 'The software that lets the model act. Claude Code is one.'],
  ['Runner', 'Where the harness executes: someone’s laptop, or a cloud machine.'],
  ['Interactions', 'Email, Slack, web chat, and the applications people already use.'],
]

---
<Base
  title="Canopy — the open source control plane for agent oversight"
  description="Give an agent deep expertise, let people use it, and let a person step in to verify before it answers. Open source, by Dimagi."
>
  <section class="hero">
    <Mark class="hero-mark" />
    <div class="wrap" style="position:relative">
      <p class="eyebrow">Open source · by Dimagi</p>
      <h1>The open source control plane for <em>agent oversight</em></h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Give an agent deep expertise, let people use it where they already work, and let a
        person step in to verify before it answers. When they do, the fix improves the agent
        itself, so the next person gets the better version too.
      </p>
      <div class="actions">
        <button class="btn btn-primary" type="button" data-beta-open>Request access</button>
        <a class="btn btn-ghost" href={GITHUB_URL}>View on GitHub</a>
      </div>
      <p class="beta-note">Access is currently closed to Dimagi and its partners. Already have access? <a href={SIGN_IN_URL} style="color:var(--on-dark)">Sign in</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <p class="eyebrow">The problem</p>
        <h2>Most AI tools are built for one person and one chat</h2>
        <p>
          That covers the first steps of getting better with AI. Nearly everything that matters
          to an organisation comes after: improving the AI itself, and then improving it together,
          so an improvement one person makes is available to everyone.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="ladder" role="list">
        {rungs.map((label, i) => (
          <div class={`rung${i >= 3 ? ' us' : ''}`} role="listitem">
            <b>{i + 1}</b>
            <span>{label}</span>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
      <div class="ladder-legend" aria-hidden="true"><span>Me and AI</span><span>Us and AI · where Canopy works</span></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-dark">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <p class="eyebrow">How it fits together</p>
        <h2>A control plane, the way CI or Kubernetes is one</h2>
        <p>
          Canopy decides what runs, when and where; runners execute it. Agents hand work to each
          other through the same boards people use, so every handoff is visible and a person can
          take it over.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="stack">
        {layers.map(([name, what]) => (
          <div class="layer"><h3>{name}</h3><p>{what}</p></div>
        ))}
      </div>
      <div class="bands">
        <div class="band band-guard"><b>Guardrails &amp; verification</b><span>Hooks that make the wrong path impossible, and checks anchored outside the model’s own work.</span></div>
        <div class="band band-loop"><b>The learning loop</b><span>People see each other’s sessions, and AI reviews those sessions to propose improvements to the system.</span></div>
      </div>
      <p class="stack-note">The bands are not layers: guardrails show up in the context, the harness, the runner and every interaction.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <p class="eyebrow">What makes it different</p>
        <h2>Oversight that teaches, and agents that keep the lesson</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="three">
        <article class="card">
          <h3>Multiplayer sessions</h3>
          <p>
            Several people and an agent in one live conversation. Each person has their own box,
            every message shows who wrote it, and the agent’s steps stream to everyone. An expert
            can watch and step in mid-task; others learn by watching.
          </p>
          <p class="fact">Embeddable in another application</p>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <h3>Agents that improve as they run</h3>
          <p>
            An improvement changes the agent’s skills and rules, not just one reply, and is there
            for every run after. Canopy shows each skill’s history, so the improvement is visible.
          </p>
          <p class="fact">Every skill’s history, replayable</p>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <h3>Hard limits live in code</h3>
          <p>
            A person sets each agent to manual or auto; the agent cannot change it. Rules are enforced
            by hooks, each pinned by a test. Each agent has its own identity and secrets, and callers
            are not trusted by default.
          </p>
          <p class="fact">Every rule came from something that went wrong</p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-stone">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <p class="eyebrow">Who it’s for</p>
        <h2>Built for everyone around the agent, not only its builder</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="people">
        <div class="person">
          <h3>Agent builders and reviewers</h3>
          <p>Build the agents, write their skills and rules, review what they did, and approve the fixes.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="person">
          <h3>People who ask for work</h3>
          <p>Reach an agent by email, Slack, web chat or an application, with nothing to install.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="person">
          <h3>Learners and viewers</h3>
          <p>See how the person and the agent work together, and learn what you can do yourself.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap split">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">What needs a person</p>
        <h2 style="font-size:clamp(28px,3.6vw,38px);font-weight:600;margin-top:12px">Every decision that needs a person, in one place</h2>
        <ul class="ticks">
          <li>Agents bring proposals, not surprises: implement, skip or defer.</li>
          <li>One review can roll a fix out to several agents at once.</li>
          <li>A question waits for an answer before the agent acts.</li>
          <li>It works on a phone, so the person is not the bottleneck.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div>
        <div class="inbox" aria-label="Illustration of the Supervisor inbox">
          <div class="item">
            <small>Review · from a session review</small>
            <p>Block dry-run writes for every agent that has the tool</p>
            <div class="acts"><span>Implement</span><span>Skip</span><span>Defer</span></div>
          </div>
          <div class="item">
            <small>Question · waiting on you</small>
            <p>Which funder contact should this draft go to?</p>
            <div class="acts"><span>Answer</span></div>
          </div>
          <div class="item">
            <small>Review · draft reply</small>
            <p>Reply to the partner thread is ready to send</p>
            <div class="acts"><span>Approve</span><span>Edit</span></div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="inbox-caption">An illustration of the Supervisor inbox, with example items.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="cta">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Help us build oversight that makes AI better for everyone</h2>
      <p>Canopy is open source, with access currently closed to Dimagi and its partners. Read the code, or request access.</p>
      <div class="actions">
        <button class="btn btn-primary" type="button" data-beta-open>Request access</button>
        <a class="btn btn-ghost" href={GITHUB_URL}>View on GitHub</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

